4. Different Architectural Approaches for Your Notebook Pipeline
Depending on how your 49+ files are structured and how you want to validate the output, you can choose between three distinct approaches:
 * Approach 1: Bottom-Up Atomic Subsumption (PRIM \rightarrow COMP — Recommended)
   * How it works: You use the notebook to first translate the lowest-level atomic primitives (PRIM—scaling blocks, motor/valve drivers, UDTs/DBs) and save those approved Altus blocks back into the notebook as [SEMANTIC_] reference sources. Next, you translate the higher-level orchestrating blocks (COMP—sequencers and OB1 routines), which simply call the already-verified atomic blocks via hierarchical subsumption.
   * Pros: Eliminates repetitive errors, prevents AI hallucination on nested calls, and mirrors the modular task bundling proven in your maintenance ontology.
 * Approach 2: LSM-Tree Batch Compaction (Best if the 49+ PDFs are fragmented or noisy)
   * How it works: Following the N=30 LSM-Tree Compaction strategy from your AI-CORE framework, you group the 49+ files into two logical batches (e.g., Batch A: Hardware, I/O, DBs, and Low-Level FCs; Batch B: Process FBs, PID loops, and Comms). After processing each batch, you run a "Compaction Pass" that consolidates the extracted logic into a clean, low-token Markdown summary (CompressedEnglishLowToken / SYS-KNOW-01) before generating the final Altus code.
   * Pros: Overcomes PDF formatting noise (headers, footers, split rungs across pages) and keeps retrieval latency ultra-low.
 * Approach 3: Multi-Agent Blackboard Verification (SYS-MEM-01 & Belief Deltas)
   * How it works: You use two separate prompt roles against the same notebook—Prompt Role A ("The Transpiler") converts the Siemens logic into Altus ST/Ladder, while Prompt Role B ("The Deontic Auditor") independently compares the new Altus code against the original Siemens PDF and flags any discrepancy ("Belief Delta") in scan-time behavior, Profibus-DP fault handling, or NR-12 safety interlocks.
   * Pros: Highest reliability for safety-critical machinery where a missed normally-closed contact or edge trigger could cause equipment damage.
